[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Resolvers Architecture & Context in GraphQL"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Understanding how GraphQL executes queries requires a deep dive into resolver architecture and the context object. Resolvers are the backbone of a GraphQL server, responsible for fetching the actual data for each field defined in your schema."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"What is a Resolver?"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"A resolver is a set of functions that generate values for a GraphQL schema. Every field in a GraphQL type is backed by a resolver function. If you do not provide a custom resolver, GraphQL provides a default one that looks for a property on the parent object with the same name as the field."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"const resolvers = {
  Query: {
    user: (parent, args, context, info) => {
      return context.db.findUserById(args.id);
    }
  }
};"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"Anatomy of Resolver Arguments"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"Every resolver function receives four positional arguments. Mastering these arguments is crucial for building robust GraphQL APIs."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"parent (or source): The result of the previous resolver in the execution chain. For root-level Query fields, this is usually empty or undefined."},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"args: An object containing all GraphQL arguments passed into the field, such as (id: "123")."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"context: A shared object passed to every resolver in the request chain. Essential for authentication, database connectors, and request-specific state."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"info: Contains field-specific information relevant to the current query execution state, including schema details and field paths."},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"The Role of Context"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"The context object is instantiated per request. It acts as a middleware bridge, carrying crucial utilities and user data from the HTTP layer down to the resolver layer. This ensures that database loaders, user sessions, and caching mechanisms remain isolated per request."},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"const server = new ApolloServer({
  typeDefs,
  resolvers,
  context: ({ req }) => {
    const token = req.headers.authorization || "";
    const user = getUserFromToken(token);
    return { user, db: databaseConnectors };
  }
});"},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Handling the N+1 Problem"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"One of the architectural challenges in GraphQL is the N+1 database query problem, which occurs when resolvers fetch child entities in a loop. To solve this, developers use batching and caching mechanisms like DataLoader, typically instantiated within the context object so it persists for a single request."},{"order":17,"type":"code","text":"context: () => ({
  userLoader: new DataLoader(async (keys) => batchUsers(keys))
})"}]
